            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"To set apart as holy for God, and/or the ongoing process of moral purification and spiritual transformation (progressive sanctification).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"To remove from common status and place under an owner's claim for an appointed purpose, or to honor as holy; explicitly rejecting 'progressive inward renewal' as the definition.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Setting apart for sacred use and/or progressively making morally holy.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Placing under an owner's claim (positional/relational) and acknowledging as holy (hallowing).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus broadly defines sanctification to include both positional setting-apart and progressive moral transformation. The book restricts the definition strictly to positional claiming and hallowing, expressly excluding progressive inward renewal from the meaning of the term.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book explicitly denies that the term 'sanctification' means the progressive inward renewal by which believers grow in righteousness, a meaning that serves as the primary or dominant definition in much of the consensus.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus identifies sanctification as encompassing positional, progressive, and hallowing senses. The book accepts the positional ('owner's claim') and hallowing ('treat as holy') senses but strips away the progressive moral definition, refining the term to a purely relational and declarative scope.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"1 Thessalonians 4:3-7 directly connects sanctification to abstaining from sexual immorality and possessing one's vessel in honor, heavily implying that sanctification entails behavioral and moral change, not merely a positional claim.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
